A||||7||Lance||T-Tops?||||2||
Z||000000||Lance||02-18-2000||01:16 AM||||I am thinking of puting t-tops on my baby. But I was told that aftermarket t-tops leak real bad. Is this true? I would like to hear from other Fiero owners who installed t-tops. Do they really leak that bad?<p>[This message has been edited by Lance (edited 02-18-2000).]||||reg||2
Z||000001||Phaeton||02-18-2000||01:43 AM||||When I lived in Juneau my factory t-tops leaked, but it rains over two hundred inches per year there.<BR>I'm in a semi-arid zone now and they don't leak under occasional rain.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||02-18-2000||03:37 AM||||I don't claim to be an expert on T-tops, but I would imagine that it isn't the T-tops that are the problem, but the installation.  If you install them correctly, I wouldn't think that they'd leak (and vice-versa).  BTW--The only company that I've ever heard of that made T-tops for the Fiero was C & C (sold out to another company now??). ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Tom88gt||02-18-2000||01:53 PM||||FYI,<P>All t-top Fieros are aftermarket installed by C&C.  Even the '88 "factory" option t-tops were done by C&C.  C&C is now out of business.  <P>There are still some NOS t-top kits floating around but I've heard that prices start at $2000 w/o install.  <P>Another option is to buy a used t-top of a junkyard car (t-top Fieros rarely end up in junkyards) and have it installed on your car.  Just use the donor car's roof frame as a template for cutting your roof.  There is nothing else to do.  No subframe or anything.  Just make sure you get all the donor parts.  <P>There is a company who supposedly bought all the remaining stock of t-top parts but if you buy all the individual parts they have available it is way over $2000(about $3000 I think) and still no roof frame (the most important part).<P>I saw an almost complete junkyard t-top roof on E-bay last week.  Starting price was $1700.  But no bidders at the time.<P>My '88 "official factory" C&C t-tops don't leak, but I havn't been through the car wash yet.<P>If you want t-tops, first I suggest you consider buying a t-top car, if you can find one for sale you can afford (SXM is currently selling a very nice '88 for $6900.  Someone recently had an '85 for $1000 or so but it didn't run).  Second, try the junkyard route (don't be afraid of having your roof cut, they were done that way).  Third, buy one of the few NOS kits still out there (but you still got to install it).<P>Good luck,<BR>Tom||||reg||1
Z||000004||88 Database Nut||02-18-2000||02:17 PM||||Guys, there's a dude named David out in CA,<BR>who has a great parts business running.  I haven't bought anything lately 'cause I didn't need anything, but right now he has complete ttop kits with tops for $500.  Yes<BR>you read it right, $500 for the whole shebang, which are from parts cars he has apparently cut the ttop roofs off of.  The url is:  cyberverse.com/~cyco/fieroparts||||reg||3
Z||000005||mrfiero||02-18-2000||03:08 PM||||A couple of comments.....<P>First, C&C is not out of business.  They merely changed their name to Farmont Skylite (probably merged with another company).  It is true that they no longer stock Fiero stuff, but up until a few months ago you could.  Fiero Parts & Appraisals bought out their remaining Fiero stock.  I imagine that Farmont (C&C) would have eventually thrown it all away, so at least the stuff is still around.<P>Second, you can install a used set of T-Tops onto your Fiero, but it isn't an easy job and you will still need p-seals and weatherseals.  P-seals cannot be reused, but the weatherseals can, assuming they were removed properly and not cut in half.  I would personally rather buy a Fiero with T-Tops already installed versus trying to install a set....it will be easier and cheaper in the long run.  If you look hard enough you can find T-Top Fieros pretty cheap.  Even if they don't run it's easier to fix the mechanicals than install the T-Tops.<P>That brings me to my third comment....I am the owner of that non-running '85 SE w/T-Tops ($1000).  I believe all that's wrong with it is a bad pick-up coil, but since the car is 700 miles away from me (at my sisters house) I can't tinker with it.   I am open to reasonable offers, but it won't be much lower than $1000.  <P>Even if you bought the used kit for $500 it would still cost you at least another $500 (probably more) to install them....factor in the cost of new p-seals and whatnot and you can see that it would be cheaper in the long run to buy a Fiero with the T-Tops already installed then transfer your parts to that frame.           <P>Sorry to be so long winded, but I have owned several T-Top Fieros and I even had a set installed on a Formula that I owned years ago.  That install cost me almost $3000...including the price of the T-Tops themselves.  In comparison, I bought my latest "factory" T-Top Formula for a few bucks more than that.  It just isn't worth the expense (in my opinion, of course).<P>If anyone is interested in my '85 SE please e-mail me privately.....I'm also selling my T-Top Formula too.  E-mail me for more details on either one.   ||||reg||6
Z||000006||Lance||02-18-2000||04:05 PM||||Thanx for all the input. I think I'll just get a Fiero with t-tops already installed, it sounds cheaper and less of a hassle. I live in an apartment and there is no real way for me to that kind of work anyway. Thanx again for the info.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
